NNAAMMEE
       loop, loop‐control - loop devices

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       #include <linux/loop.h>

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  loop  device  is a block device that maps its data blocks not to a
       physical device such as a hard disk or optical disk drive, but  to  the
       blocks  of  a  regular file in a filesystem or to another block device.
       This can be useful for example to provide a block device for a filesys‐
       tem image stored in a file, so that it can be mounted with the mmoouunntt(8)
       command.  You could do

           $ dddd iiff==//ddeevv//zzeerroo ooff==ffiillee..iimmgg bbss==11MMiiBB ccoouunntt==1100;
           $ ssuuddoo lloosseettuupp //ddeevv//lloooopp44 ffiillee..iimmgg;
           $ ssuuddoo mmkkffss --tt eexxtt44 //ddeevv//lloooopp44;
           $ ssuuddoo mmkkddiirr //mmyyllooooppddeevv;
           $ ssuuddoo mmoouunntt //ddeevv//lloooopp44 //mmyyllooooppddeevv;

       See lloosseettuupp(8) for another example.

       A transfer function can be specified for each loop device  for  encryp‐
       tion and decryption purposes.

       The  following  iiooccttll(2)  operations are provided by the loop block de‐
       vice:

       LLOOOOPP__SSEETT__FFDD
              Associate the loop device with the open file whose file descrip‐
              tor is passed as the (third) iiooccttll(2) argument.

       LLOOOOPP__CCLLRR__FFDD
              Disassociate the loop device from any file descriptor.

       LLOOOOPP__SSEETT__SSTTAATTUUSS
              Set the status of the loop device using the (third) iiooccttll(2) ar‐
              gument.  This argument is a pointer to  a  _l_o_o_p___i_n_f_o  structure,
              defined in _<_l_i_n_u_x_/_l_o_o_p_._h_> as:

                  struct loop_info {
                      int           lo_number;      /* ioctl r/o */
                      dev_t         lo_device;      /* ioctl r/o */
                      unsigned long lo_inode;       /* ioctl r/o */
                      dev_t         lo_rdevice;     /* ioctl r/o */
                      int           lo_offset;
                      int           lo_encrypt_type;
                      int           lo_encrypt_key_size;  /* ioctl w/o */
                      int           lo_flags;       /* ioctl r/w (r/o before
                                                       Linux 2.6.25) */
                      char          lo_name[LO_NAME_SIZE];
                      unsigned char lo_encrypt_key[LO_KEY_SIZE];
                                                    /* ioctl w/o */
                      unsigned long lo_init[2];
                      char          reserved[4];
                  };

              The   encryption   type   (_l_o___e_n_c_r_y_p_t___t_y_p_e)  should  be  one  of
              LLOO__CCRRYYPPTT__NNOONNEE,   LLOO__CCRRYYPPTT__XXOORR,   LLOO__CCRRYYPPTT__DDEESS,   LLOO__CCRRYYPPTT__FFIISSHH22,
              LLOO__CCRRYYPPTT__BBLLOOWW,  LLOO__CCRRYYPPTT__CCAASSTT112288, LLOO__CCRRYYPPTT__IIDDEEAA, LLOO__CCRRYYPPTT__DDUUMMMMYY,
              LLOO__CCRRYYPPTT__SSKKIIPPJJAACCKK, or (since Linux 2.6.0) LLOO__CCRRYYPPTT__CCRRYYPPTTOOAAPPII.

              The _l_o___f_l_a_g_s field is a bit mask that can include zero  or  more
              of the following:

              LLOO__FFLLAAGGSS__RREEAADD__OONNLLYY
                     The loopback device is read‐only.

              LLOO__FFLLAAGGSS__AAUUTTOOCCLLEEAARR (since Linux 2.6.25)
                     The loopback device will autodestruct on last close.

              LLOO__FFLLAAGGSS__PPAARRTTSSCCAANN (since Linux 3.2)
                     Allow automatic partition scanning.

              LLOO__FFLLAAGGSS__DDIIRREECCTT__IIOO (since Linux 4.10)
                     Use direct I/O mode to access the backing file.

              The  only  _l_o___f_l_a_g_s  that can be modified by LLOOOOPP__SSEETT__SSTTAATTUUSS are
              LLOO__FFLLAAGGSS__AAUUTTOOCCLLEEAARR and LLOO__FFLLAAGGSS__PPAARRTTSSCCAANN.

       LLOOOOPP__GGEETT__SSTTAATTUUSS
              Get the status of the loop device.  The (third)  iiooccttll(2)  argu‐
              ment must be a pointer to a _s_t_r_u_c_t _l_o_o_p___i_n_f_o.

       LLOOOOPP__CCHHAANNGGEE__FFDD (since Linux 2.6.5)
              Switch  the  backing  store  of  the loop device to the new file
              identified file descriptor specified in the (third) iiooccttll(2) ar‐
              gument, which is an integer.  This operation is possible only if
              the loop device is read‐only and the new backing  store  is  the
              same size and type as the old backing store.

       LLOOOOPP__SSEETT__CCAAPPAACCIITTYY (since Linux 2.6.30)
              Resize  a  live loop device.  One can change the size of the un‐
              derlying backing store and then use this operation so  that  the
              loop  driver learns about the new size.  This operation takes no
              argument.

       LLOOOOPP__SSEETT__DDIIRREECCTT__IIOO (since Linux 4.10)
              Set DIRECT I/O mode on the loop device, so that it can  be  used
              to  open  backing file.  The (third) iiooccttll(2) argument is an un‐
              signed long value.  A nonzero represents direct I/O mode.

       LLOOOOPP__SSEETT__BBLLOOCCKK__SSIIZZEE (since Linux 4.14)
              Set the block size of the loop device.  The (third) iiooccttll(2) ar‐
              gument is an unsigned long value.  This value must be a power of
              two in the range [512,pagesize]; otherwise, an EEIINNVVAALL error  re‐
              sults.

       LLOOOOPP__CCOONNFFIIGGUURREE (since Linux 5.8)
              Setup  and configure all loop device parameters in a single step
              using the (third) iiooccttll(2) argument.  This argument is a pointer
              to a _l_o_o_p___c_o_n_f_i_g structure, defined in _<_l_i_n_u_x_/_l_o_o_p_._h_> as:

                  struct loop_config {
                      __u32               fd;
                      __u32               block_size;
                      struct loop_info64  info;
                      __u64               __reserved[8];
                  };

              In addition to doing what LLOOOOPP__SSEETT__SSTTAATTUUSS can do, LLOOOOPP__CCOONNFFIIGGUURREE
              can also be used to do the following:

              •  set the correct block size immediately by  setting  _l_o_o_p___c_o_n_‐
                 _f_i_g_._b_l_o_c_k___s_i_z_e;

              •  explicitly  request  direct  I/O mode by setting LLOO__FFLLAAGGSS__DDII‐‐
                 RREECCTT__IIOO in _l_o_o_p___c_o_n_f_i_g_._i_n_f_o_._l_o___f_l_a_g_s; and

              •  explicitly    request    read‐only    mode     by     setting
                 LLOO__FFLLAAGGSS__RREEAADD__OONNLLYY in _l_o_o_p___c_o_n_f_i_g_._i_n_f_o_._l_o___f_l_a_g_s.

       Since Linux 2.6, there are two new iiooccttll(2) operations:

       LLOOOOPP__SSEETT__SSTTAATTUUSS6644
